--- does_app_diagnosis_md_explain_why_resta ---
{"score": "yes", "reasoning": "The \"Restarting servers\" section explains that a restart restores persisted/agreed state, does not undo downstream skip records or watermarks, and leaves the faulty delivery path in the binary. It adds that a restart can itself expose the discrepancy and that a quiet restart might only avoid the race on one run. This says the defect is in the delivery/reset accounting code and not in transient runtime state, which matches the reference."}

--- does_app_diagnosis_md_explain_why_purgi ---
{"score": "yes", "reasoning": "The diagnosis has a section \"Why operational shortcuts are not repairs\". It explains that purging, truncating or re-reading downstream destroys stored content and doesn't fix the protocol, so the failure could recur at the next transition. It also says re-reading doesn't fill recorded holes because of the watermark. For deleting and recreating the consumer, it says the same flow-controlled replicated delivery code can repeat the failure, and it abandons durable state. For deleting and recreating the mirror or source, it calls this replacement that leaves the defect in the broker. These points match the reference's reasoning that the defect recurs and the objects must stay the same."}

--- does_app_diagnosis_md_explain_why_turni ---
{"score": "yes", "reasoning": "The \"Turning off consumer flow control\" section says AckFlowControl requires flow control, so disabling it isn't permitted. It also says that disabling flow control \"does not repair the reset barrier and is not a repair of the required durable flow-controlled path,\" and that the corrected path keeps flow control enabled. This matches the criterion: the reset race is independent of flow control, and flow control must stay active. The diagnosis also says the flow-control exception is one cause, so the explanation is a little muddied. It does still state that disabling flow control wouldn't fix the reset race, and that flow control must stay on."}

